# Bugfix And Perf Change Playbook

When the task is a traceback fix, timeout fix, memory/runtime reduction, or operational reliability issue, follow this implementation playbook.

## Implementation defaults

- Start from the concrete failure signature (traceback line, SQL timeout, or log message) and patch the smallest root-cause path first.
- If the failure is not fixed after the first request, deep dive into the code and find the systemic failure. Don't patch the symptoms. Do root cause analysis first.
- Preserve existing user-facing behavior unless the user asks for behavior changes.
- Make resource/concurrency choices configuration-driven (thread count, batch size, retry policy, timeout), not hidden magic constants. If hardcoded fallback defaults are required, keep them centralized and documented.
- Prefer deterministic fallback paths over silent failures (explicit no-data reason, explicit retry boundary, clear error branch).
- Keep code paths composable and testable; avoid adding one-off branches that bypass canonical helpers.

## Verification defaults

- Add or extend targeted regression tests for the specific failure mode.
- For DB/Redis/networked behavior, validate in compose-network workflows, not host-only assumptions.
- When fix scope changes test commands or runner prerequisites, update `TESTING.md` in the same task.

## Guardrails

- Do not introduce destructive cleanup behavior (deleting live data, force resets) unless explicitly requested.
- When changing cache/perf behavior, verify invalidation and stale-data boundaries, not just raw speed.

